From 51305ce52fca8fac27c92d7daf9e60785a5542a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 11:59:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: add India geography master data (countries -> postal
 codes)

New global reference tables -- countries, states, districts,
sub_districts, cities, postal_codes -- and a seed script that actually
populates them with real data, not placeholders.

Models (src/models/country.model.js, state.model.js, district.model.js,
subDistrict.model.js, city.model.js, postalCode.model.js):
- Deliberately NOT tenant-scoped, unlike every other model in this
  codebase -- a district is the same row regardless of which
  organization/tenant is looking it up. Wired into models/index.js as
  a straight Country -> State -> District -> SubDistrict -> City ->
  PostalCode hasMany/belongsTo chain.
- City.name covers both cities and villages (the source data doesn't
  distinguish them -- a "city" here is whatever a post office serves).
  raw_office_name/office_type are kept alongside the cleaned name for
  traceability back to the source row.
- PostalCode is modeled many-to-one from City, not a forced 1:1, since
  dense areas can have multiple pincodes for one locality.
- Facility does NOT yet FK into this chain -- it still stores
  city/state/district as free text. Pointing it at these tables means
  reconciling every existing facility's free-text values against real
  rows first, which is a separate, larger migration, not something to
  do silently as a side effect of adding the reference tables.

Data (seed-data/india-geo/, ~9.6MB of CSVs, see the README in that
folder for full provenance):
- countries.csv: all 249 current ISO 3166-1 entries.
- states/districts/sub_districts/cities/postal_codes.csv: derived from
  India Post's "All India Pincode Directory" (data.gov.in, Government
  Open Data License - India), ~154,800 post offices normalized into
  this schema. Two known post-2017 reorganizations that source predates
  were corrected during import: the Oct 2019 Jammu & Kashmir / Ladakh
  split (by Leh/Kargil district) and the Jan 2020 Dadra & Nagar Haveli
  / Daman & Diu merger. Verified the result lands on exactly 36
  states/UTs, matching India's current count.
- Ordinary district-level boundary changes since 2017 were NOT
  reconciled (India splits districts fairly often) -- the README flags
  this clearly: solid for address entry/lookup, not a substitute for
  LGD (Local Government Directory) codes where those are specifically
  required.

scripts/seed-india-geo.js: reads the CSVs and bulk-inserts them
(batched, 2000 rows/batch) into whichever DB the app is pointed at.
Idempotent -- skips a table if it already has rows rather than
duplicating. Added as `npm run seed:india-geo`. Added csv-parse as a
dependency since several generated fields (city/sub-district names)
contain commas that a naive split(',') would mis-parse.

Verified end-to-end against a real (file-backed, not :memory:) sqlite
DB: seeded all ~320k rows, then resolved pincode 400050 back up the
full chain to Bandra West -> Mumbai -> Mumbai -> Maharashtra -> India,
correctly. Confirmed exactly 1 Jammu and Kashmir row and 1 Ladakh row
post-correction. Full existing test suite (18 tests) still passes
unchanged.

Not included in this commit, left as natural next steps: read-only
list/lookup API endpoints (GET /countries, /states?countryId=,
/districts?stateId=, etc.) for cascading address-picker dropdowns, and
the Facility free-text-to-FK migration mentioned above.
